const CASTLES = [
  {"name":"Odawara Castle","modern":"Odawara, Kanagawa Prefecture","type":"hirayamajiro","clan":"Go-Hojo (Later Hojo)","lat":35.2510472,"lon":139.1534417,"note":"Odawara was fortified from the Kamakura period and passed in 1495 to Ise Moritoki, founder of the Odawara Hōjō. Five generations of the Hōjō turned it into the administrative centre of a domain covering most of the Kantō, with water moats on the low side, dry ditches on the hill side, banks and cliffs that turned back Uesugi Kenshin in 1561 and Takeda Shingen in 1569. Toyotomi Hideyoshi broke it in 1590 with a three month siege and bluff rather than an assault, then ordered most of the works razed. The Edo period castle rebuilt by Ōkubo Tadayo fitted entirely inside what had been the third bailey of the Hōjō fortress; its keep was pulled down between 1870 and 1872, and the present concrete keep dates from 1960.","images":[{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b0/Odawara-jo.jpg/960px-Odawara-jo.jpg","caption":"The keep of Odawara Castle, rebuilt in reinforced concrete in 1960 on the original stone base, Wikimedia Commons."}],"sources":[{"label":"Wikipedia: Odawara Castle","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Odawara_Castle"}]},
  {"name":"Edo Castle","modern":"Chiyoda, Tokyo","type":"hirayamajiro","clan":"Tokugawa (built by Ota Dokan)","lat":35.688325,"lon":139.7543667,"note":"Edo Castle was begun in 1457 by Ōta Dōkan in Toshima District of Musashi Province and was taken by Hōjō Ujitsuna in 1524. Tokugawa Ieyasu made it his seat after 1590 and, from 1603, the headquarters of the shogunate, which it remained until 1867. Its outermost moat enclosed ground now occupied by Tokyo Station, the Marunouchi district and Kitanomaru Park, an area far larger than the present palace grounds. After the Meiji Restoration the site became the Tokyo Imperial Palace, and moats, ramparts and stretches of wall survive.","images":[{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5f/Edo_P_detail.jpg/960px-Edo_P_detail.jpg","caption":"Detail of the seventeenth century Edo zu folding screens showing the moats and baileys of Edo Castle, Wikimedia Commons."}],"sources":[{"label":"Wikipedia: Edo Castle","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Edo_Castle"}]},
  {"name":"Kawagoe Castle","modern":"Kawagoe, Saitama Prefecture","type":"hirayamajiro","clan":"Ogigayatsu Uesugi / Go-Hojo","lat":35.9245139,"lon":139.4915028,"note":"Kawagoe stood at the centre of the fifteenth and sixteenth century contest for the Kantō between the Late Hōjō and the two branches of the Uesugi. Hōjō Ujitsuna seized it in 1537, thirteen years after taking Edo, and it became a forward base commanding the road to Echigo and the line of the Arakawa. The Uesugi attempt to recover it ended in the celebrated night battle at Kawagoe, after which Uesugi power in the region collapsed and Kawagoe served for some forty five years as a satellite fortress screening Edo and Odawara. It was the seat of Kawagoe Domain throughout the Edo period; dismantling began in 1870, leaving the Honmaru Goten and a yagura mound on the original site.","images":[{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/6b/Kawagoejou.jpg/960px-Kawagoejou.jpg","caption":"The surviving Honmaru Goten of Kawagoe Castle, with its curved karahafu entrance porch, Wikimedia Commons."}],"sources":[{"label":"Wikipedia: Kawagoe Castle","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kawagoe_Castle"}]},
  {"name":"Hachigata Castle","modern":"Yorii, Osato District, Saitama Prefecture","type":"hirayamajiro","clan":"Go-Hojo (Hojo Ujikuni)","lat":36.1097333,"lon":139.1959833,"note":"Hachigata was built about 1476 by Nagao Kageharu on a spur enclosed by the Arakawa and Fukasawa rivers, and its position at the western edge of the Kantō plain made it a key to Musashi Province. Hōjō Ujikuni took charge of the northern Hōjō holdings in 1564 and rebuilt it with wide dry moats and clay ramparts at the foot of the hill, using stone facing at the main gate as a display of authority; the defences eventually ran nearly a kilometre along the ridge. Takeda Shingen failed to take it in 1568. In 1590 Ujikuni held out for a month with roughly 3,000 men against some 30,000 under Maeda Toshiie and Uesugi Kagekatsu before surrendering on terms that spared his garrison, after which the castle was demolished.","images":[{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/ea/Reconstructed_gate_of_Hachigata.jpg/960px-Reconstructed_gate_of_Hachigata.jpg","caption":"The reconstructed gate and earthen wall at the Hachigata Castle site in Yorii, Saitama, Wikimedia Commons."}],"sources":[{"label":"Wikipedia: Hachigata Castle","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hachigata_Castle"}]},
  {"name":"Hachioji Castle","modern":"Hachioji, Tokyo","type":"yamashiro","clan":"Go-Hojo (Hojo Ujiteru)","lat":35.6529333,"lon":139.2521139,"note":"Hōjō Ujiteru began Hachiōji Castle on Mount Fukasawa in the 1570s, after Takeda troops nearly overran his earlier seat at Takiyama in 1569, and he moved his household there by 1587. It guarded the Kobotoke and Ange passes on the road between Kai and Musashi, with a stone lined approach and a drawbridge before the main gate, yet the hilltop inner bailey was small and barely defended, evidence that the work was never finished. In 1590 Ujiteru was away at Odawara and left about 1,300 defenders; the Toyotomi detachment under Maeda Toshiie and Uesugi Kagekatsu attacked at dawn on 23 June and took the castle within the day. Tokugawa Ieyasu ordered the site destroyed and it was shunned for years as haunted; the stone walls, moat bridge and residence entrance seen today were reconstructed in 1990.","images":[{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/2e/Hachioji_Castle_Gosyuden_entrance.JPG/960px-Hachioji_Castle_Gosyuden_entrance.JPG","caption":"The restored stone stairway and gate posts at the entrance to the Goshuden, the lord's residence compound at Hachiōji Castle, Wikimedia Commons."}],"sources":[{"label":"Wikipedia: Hachiōji Castle","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hachi%C5%8Dji_Castle"}]},
  {"name":"Kasugayama Castle","modern":"Joetsu, Niigata Prefecture","type":"yamashiro","clan":"Nagao / Uesugi","lat":37.1466889,"lon":138.2055528,"note":"Kasugayama was a yamashiro spread over two ridges of the 180 metre Mount Kasuga, built without stone walls and relying instead on terraced enclosures with earthen ramparts and dry moats. Probably raised by Nagao Tamekage, it passed in 1548 to Uesugi Kenshin and served as his base for the Kawanakajima campaigns against Takeda Shingen. Uesugi Kagekatsu secured it after the succession struggle that followed Kenshin's death in 1578, and when the Uesugi were transferred to Aizu in 1598 the castle went to Hori Hideharu, who moved his seat to Fukushima Castle near the port of Naoetsu in 1600. Kasugayama was deserted by 1607 and has been a National Historic Site since 1935.","images":[{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e9/Horikiri_style_moat_of_Kasugayama_Castle.jpg/960px-Horikiri_style_moat_of_Kasugayama_Castle.jpg","caption":"A horikiri, a defensive trench cut across the ridge, surviving on the Kasugayama Castle site at Jōetsu, Wikimedia Commons."}],"sources":[{"label":"Wikipedia: Kasugayama Castle","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kasugayama_Castle"}]},
  {"name":"Numata Castle","modern":"Numata, Gunma Prefecture","type":"hirayamajiro","clan":"Sanada","lat":36.648722,"lon":139.039028,"note":"Strategic castle on a plateau controlling the Mikuni route between Kozuke and Echigo; the disputed Numata territory between the Sanada and Hojo was a direct trigger of Hideyoshi's 1590 Odawara campaign. Japanese sources class it as a hill castle (okajiro).","images":[{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/94/Numatajyo.jpg/960px-Numatajyo.jpg","caption":"The stone revetment and steps of the west turret base at the Numata Castle site, Numata, Gunma Prefecture. Wikimedia Commons."}],"sources":[{"label":"Wikipedia: Numata Castle","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Numata_Castle"}]},
  {"name":"Minowa Castle","modern":"Takasaki, Gunma Prefecture","type":"hirayamajiro","clan":"Nagano (later Takeda, then Ii)","lat":36.404944,"lon":138.950972,"note":"Stronghold of the Nagano clan under the renowned Nagano Narimasa, who long resisted Takeda Shingen's invasions of western Kozuke; finally fell to Shingen in 1566 and later passed to Ii Naomasa. One of the Top 100 Castles of Japan.","images":[{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a3/Minowajo_kakuumadashi_nishikoguchimon.jpg/960px-Minowajo_kakuumadashi_nishikoguchimon.jpg","caption":"The Kaku-umadashi Nishi-koguchi gate of Minowa Castle in Takasaki, Gunma Prefecture, reconstructed in timber in 2016. Wikimedia Commons."}],"sources":[{"label":"Wikipedia: Minowa Castle","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Minowa_Castle"}]},
  {"name":"Karasawayama Castle","modern":"Sano, Tochigi Prefecture","type":"yamashiro","clan":"Sano","lat":36.353639,"lon":139.600944,"note":"Formidable mountain castle of the Sano clan in southern Shimotsuke; famed for reputedly repelling repeated attacks by Uesugi Kenshin, it sat at the contested frontier between the Uesugi, Hojo, and Satake spheres.","images":[{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/63/Karasawayama_Shrine.JPG/960px-Karasawayama_Shrine.JPG","caption":"The stone stairway and torii of Karasawayama Shrine, which stands on the honmaru of Karasawayama Castle in Sano, Tochigi Prefecture. Wikimedia Commons."}],"sources":[{"label":"Wikipedia: Karasawa Castle","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Karasawa_Castle"}]},
  {"name":"Utsunomiya Castle","modern":"Utsunomiya, Tochigi Prefecture","type":"hirajiro","clan":"Utsunomiya","lat":36.55444,"lon":139.88583,"note":"Long-held seat of the powerful Utsunomiya clan, who governed central Shimotsuke from the late Heian era (c. 1063) for roughly five centuries; a key player in the Kanto power struggles before being dispossessed by Hideyoshi in 1597.","images":[{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b6/Utsunomiya-jo_fujimi-yagura.jpg/960px-Utsunomiya-jo_fujimi-yagura.jpg","caption":"The Fujimi-yagura turret of Utsunomiya Castle, rebuilt in 2007 on the castle's earthen rampart. Wikimedia Commons."}],"sources":[{"label":"Wikipedia: Utsunomiya Castle","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Utsunomiya_Castle"}]},
  {"name":"Oyama Castle (Gion Castle)","modern":"Oyama, Tochigi Prefecture","type":"hirajiro","clan":"Oyama","lat":36.319333,"lon":139.8,"note":"Flatland castle of the Oyama clan, shugo of Shimotsuke; in 1380 it was the focus of the Oyama Yoshimasa Rebellion against the Kamakura kubo, a major Nanboku-cho/early-Muromachi conflict in the Kanto.","images":[{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/22/Oyam-jo.entrance.2.JPG/960px-Oyam-jo.entrance.2.JPG","caption":"The terraced embankments at the entrance to the Oyama Castle (Gion Castle) site, now Shiroyama Park, in Oyama, Tochigi Prefecture. Wikimedia Commons."}],"sources":[{"label":"Wikipedia: Oyama Castle","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Oyama_Castle"}]},
  {"name":"Aizu-Wakamatsu Castle (Tsuruga Castle)","modern":"Aizuwakamatsu, Fukushima Prefecture","type":"hirayamajiro","clan":"Ashina (later Date, Gamo, Uesugi)","lat":37.4877167,"lon":139.9297861,"note":"Center of the Aizu basin, seat of the Ashina clan and briefly of Date Masamune after his 1589 victory at Suriagehara; rebuilt as a major modern castle by Gamo Ujisato. Later famous for the 1868 Boshin War siege.","images":[{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e6/%E8%8B%A5%E6%9D%BE%E5%9F%8E%EF%BC%88%E9%B6%B4%E3%83%B6%E5%9F%8E%EF%BC%8920220917-P1003471.jpg/960px-%E8%8B%A5%E6%9D%BE%E5%9F%8E%EF%BC%88%E9%B6%B4%E3%83%B6%E5%9F%8E%EF%BC%8920220917-P1003471.jpg","caption":"The reconstructed keep of Aizuwakamatsu Castle (Tsuruga Castle) with its red roof tiles, seen with the corridor turret and stone walls, Aizuwakamatsu, Fukushima Prefecture. Wikimedia Commons."}],"sources":[{"label":"Wikipedia: Aizuwakamatsu Castle","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Aizuwakamatsu_Castle"}]},
  {"name":"Yonezawa Castle","modern":"Yonezawa, Yamagata Prefecture","type":"hirajiro","clan":"Date / Uesugi","lat":37.9096167,"lon":140.1051444,"note":"Yonezawa was a flatland castle in southern Dewa held first by the Nagai and then by the Date, and Date Masamune was born here in 1567. Uesugi Kagekatsu received it in 1597, and after Sekigahara cut the clan from 1,200,000 to 300,000 koku the Uesugi rebuilt it between 1608 and 1613, leaving the earthen ramparts unfaced with stone and the keep at a modest three storeys so as not to attract Tokugawa suspicion. They also moved the grave of their founder Uesugi Kenshin from Echigo to within the walls. Clan revenues were halved again in 1664, all remaining buildings were pulled down in 1873, and Uesugi Shrine now stands on the site of the keep.","images":[{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/e/e2/Yonezawajou001.jpg","caption":"The surviving water moat and stone revetment of Yonezawa Castle in Yonezawa, Yamagata Prefecture; Wikimedia Commons."}],"sources":[{"label":"Wikipedia: Yonezawa Castle","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Yonezawa_Castle"}]},
  {"name":"Yamagata Castle (Kasumigajo)","modern":"Yamagata, Yamagata Prefecture","type":"hirajiro","clan":"Mogami","lat":38.25556,"lon":140.32806,"note":"Yamagata Castle guarded the southern end of the Yamagata Basin, where the Ushu Kaido met the routes to Shonai and to Sendai, and it was the seat of the Mogami clan for roughly 275 years. Mogami Yoshiaki rebuilt it in 1592 with second and third baileys and a ring of two and three storey yagura, but no tenshu was ever raised on its prepared foundation. After Yoshiaki backed Tokugawa Ieyasu in 1600 the Mogami were raised to 570,000 koku, only to be attainted in 1622, after which a rapid succession of poorer daimyo let the main bailey fall into ruin and part of the outer bailey be ploughed up. The site is now Kajo Park, where the second bailey's Higashi Otemon was rebuilt in 1991 as part of a restoration programme running to 2033.","images":[{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/55/220430_Yamagata_Castle_Yamagata_Yamagata_pref_Japan23s3.jpg/960px-220430_Yamagata_Castle_Yamagata_Yamagata_pref_Japan23s3.jpg","caption":"The reconstructed Higashi Otemon, the east great gate of the second bailey at Yamagata Castle, rebuilt in 1991; Wikimedia Commons."}],"sources":[{"label":"Wikipedia: Yamagata Castle","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Yamagata_Castle"}]},
  {"name":"Nihonmatsu Castle (Kasumigajo)","modern":"Nihonmatsu, Fukushima Prefecture","type":"hirayamajiro","clan":"Hatakeyama (Nihonmatsu)","lat":37.5996417,"lon":140.4279861,"note":"Hatakeyama Takakuni established a fortified residence here in 1341 on a spur of the Adatara mountains commanding the Oshu Kaido, and his line took the Nihonmatsu name. In 1585 Nihonmatsu Yoshitsugu seized Date Terumune as a hostage and both men died in the Date counterattack; Date Masamune burned and then rebuilt the castle in 1587 as a border fortress against the Ashina. From 1643 the Niwa clan abandoned the hilltop enclosure and rebuilt the castle at the foot of the hill, ruling Nihonmatsu Domain from there until the Meiji Restoration. In the Boshin War of 1868 it fell in a single day to superior Sattcho firepower, with 337 Nihonmatsu samurai killed, and the surviving structures were demolished in 1872.","images":[{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/92/Nihonmatsu_Castle_Minowa_Gate_20100625-02.jpg/960px-Nihonmatsu_Castle_Minowa_Gate_20100625-02.jpg","caption":"The Minowa Gate of Nihonmatsu Castle, reconstructed together with its attached yagura in 1982; Wikimedia Commons."}],"sources":[{"label":"Wikipedia: Nihonmatsu Castle","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Nihonmatsu_Castle"}]},
  {"name":"Kubota Castle","modern":"Akita, Akita Prefecture","type":"hirayamajiro","clan":"Satake","lat":39.7234806,"lon":140.1232417,"note":"Satake Yoshinobu began Kubota Castle in 1603, after Tokugawa Ieyasu transferred the clan from its Hitachi homeland to northern Dewa, completing the main bailey in 1604 and laying out a castle town that was extended repeatedly through the 1620s and 1630s. Built on a low hill above the Nibetsu River, it relied on wet moats, earthworks and eight yagura rather than stone walls, and it never had an imposing keep, possibly to avoid attracting shogunal suspicion. Fires in 1633, 1776, 1778 and 1797 repeatedly gutted it, and after the Satake surrendered the castle in 1869 a final fire in 1880 destroyed the abandoned main bailey. The grounds became Senshu Park, where a corner yagura was reconstructed in 1989 and the front gate in 2001.","images":[{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/37/Osumi-Yagura_of_Kubota-Castle_20160424.jpg/960px-Osumi-Yagura_of_Kubota-Castle_20160424.jpg","caption":"The Osumi-yagura corner turret of Kubota Castle in Akita, reconstructed in 1989 and photographed in cherry blossom season; Wikimedia Commons."}],"sources":[{"label":"Wikipedia: Kubota Castle","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kubota_Castle"}]},
  {"name":"Azuchi Castle","modern":"Ōmihachiman (Azuchi), Shiga Prefecture","type":"yamashiro","clan":"Oda","lat":35.1559139,"lon":136.1394083,"note":"Oda Nobunaga had Azuchi built between 1576 and 1579 on Mount Azuchi above Lake Biwa, with Niwa Nagahide supervising, close enough to watch the approaches to Kyoto but clear of the capital's fires and disorder. Its keep rose seven levels, six above ground and one below, with an octagonal top storey, a facade painted with tigers and dragons and interiors by Kano Eitoku, making it as much a political display as a fortress. Nobunaga chartered a tax-free castle town below it in 1577 that held roughly 5,000 people by 1582, and staged the Azuchi religious debate there in 1579. The castle burned within weeks of his death at Honno-ji in 1582 and was never rebuilt; the stone foundations and stairways on the hill are a Special National Historic Site.","images":[{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b0/%E8%BF%91%E6%B1%9F%E5%9B%BD%E8%92%B2%E7%94%9F%E9%83%A1%E5%AE%89%E5%9C%9F%E5%9F%8E%E4%B9%8B.jpg/960px-%E8%BF%91%E6%B1%9F%E5%9B%BD%E8%92%B2%E7%94%9F%E9%83%A1%E5%AE%89%E5%9C%9F%E5%9F%8E%E4%B9%8B.jpg","caption":"A later Japanese painted bird's eye view of Azuchi Castle rising above Lake Biwa, colours on silk, Osaka Castle Museum; Wikimedia Commons."}],"sources":[{"label":"Wikipedia: Azuchi Castle","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Azuchi_Castle"}]},
  {"name":"Gifu Castle (Inabayama)","modern":"Gifu City, Gifu Prefecture","type":"yamashiro","clan":"Saitō / Oda","lat":35.4340722,"lon":136.7822139,"note":"The hill fort begun by the Nikaido in the early thirteenth century grew into Inabayama Castle, stronghold of Saito Dosan, the so-called Viper of Mino, and of his descendants. Oda Nobunaga besieged and took it in 1567, renamed both castle and town Gifu after a Chinese precedent, rebuilt it on a grander scale with a mountaintop tenshu, and used it as his main base for about ten years before moving to Azuchi and handing Gifu to his son Oda Nobutada. It fell again after a single day's fighting in 1600, in the prelude to Sekigahara, and in 1603 Okudaira Nobumasa abandoned it for the new Kano Castle at the foot of the mountain, re-erecting some of its structures there. The present keep on Mount Kinka is a 1950s concrete and steel reconstruction that replaced a 1910s wooden one destroyed by fire in 1943.","images":[{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/01/Gifu_Castle.jpg/960px-Gifu_Castle.jpg","caption":"The reconstructed keep of Gifu Castle on the summit of Mount Kinka, Gifu Prefecture; Wikimedia Commons."}],"sources":[{"label":"Wikipedia: Gifu Castle","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gifu_Castle"}]},
  {"name":"Kiyosu Castle","modern":"Kiyosu City, Aichi Prefecture","type":"hirajiro","clan":"Oda","lat":35.216575,"lon":136.8435972,"note":"Nobunaga's main Owari base from 1555; site of the 1582 Kiyosu Conference where the Oda succession was decided after Honnō-ji.","images":[{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/b/b9/Kiyosu_Castle.JPG","caption":"The 1989 replica donjon at Kiyosu, Aichi Prefecture, seen across its raked gravel garden. Wikimedia Commons."}],"sources":[{"label":"Wikipedia: Kiyosu Castle","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kiyosu_Castle"}]},
  {"name":"Inuyama Castle","modern":"Inuyama City, Aichi Prefecture","type":"hirayamajiro","clan":"Oda (織田) / Naruse","lat":35.3883222,"lon":136.9392611,"note":"Bluff castle commanding the Kiso River on the Owari–Mino border; its keep is one of Japan's twelve surviving original tenshu and a National Treasure.","images":[{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/9a/Castle_in_Inuyama.JPG/960px-Castle_in_Inuyama.JPG","caption":"The surviving tenshu of Inuyama Castle, Aichi Prefecture, framed by cherry blossom. Wikimedia Commons."}],"sources":[{"label":"Wikipedia: Inuyama Castle","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Inuyama_Castle"}]},
  {"name":"Okazaki Castle","modern":"Okazaki City, Aichi Prefecture","type":"hirayamajiro","clan":"Matsudaira / Tokugawa","lat":34.9563083,"lon":137.158806,"note":"Birthplace of Tokugawa Ieyasu (1543) and the ancestral seat of the Matsudaira clan; cradle of the Mikawa power base that grew into the Tokugawa shogunate.","images":[{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/2f/Okazaki_Castle_2023.jpg/960px-Okazaki_Castle_2023.jpg","caption":"The reconstructed donjon of Okazaki Castle, Aichi Prefecture, rising behind the pines of the castle park. Wikimedia Commons."}],"sources":[{"label":"Wikipedia: Okazaki Castle","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Okazaki_Castle"}]},
  {"name":"Hamamatsu Castle","modern":"Hamamatsu City, Shizuoka Prefecture","type":"hirayamajiro","clan":"Tokugawa","lat":34.7118,"lon":137.725,"note":"Ieyasu's stronghold in Tōtōmi for ~17 years; he marched from here to the disastrous 1572 Battle of Mikatagahara against Takeda Shingen. Later called the 'castle of success.'","images":[{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/7f/Hamamatsu_Castle_2023.jpg/960px-Hamamatsu_Castle_2023.jpg","caption":"The reconstructed donjon of Hamamatsu Castle floodlit at night above its nozura-zumi stone base. Wikimedia Commons."}],"sources":[{"label":"Wikipedia: Hamamatsu Castle","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hamamatsu_Castle"}]},
  {"name":"Sunpu Castle","modern":"Shizuoka City (Aoi Ward), Shizuoka Prefecture","type":"hirajiro","clan":"Imagawa / Tokugawa","lat":34.97917,"lon":138.38333,"note":"Heart of the Imagawa domain where the young Ieyasu was held hostage; he retired here as Ōgosho, ruling Japan from behind the scenes until his death in 1616. Coordinates approx (plain flatland site).","images":[{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/9e/Sunpu-castle_tatsumi-yagura.JPG/960px-Sunpu-castle_tatsumi-yagura.JPG","caption":"The Tatsumi Yagura corner turret of Sunpu Castle above its moat in Shizuoka. Wikimedia Commons."}],"sources":[{"label":"Wikipedia: Sunpu Castle","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sunpu_Castle"}]},
  {"name":"Tsutsujigasaki Yakata (Kōfu)","modern":"Kōfu City, Yamanashi Prefecture","type":"hirajiro","clan":"Takeda","lat":35.6868639,"lon":138.577472,"note":"Fortified residence of the Takeda for three generations (Nobutora, Shingen, Katsuyori); the administrative center of Kai, reflecting Shingen's maxim that men, not walls, are the castle. Now Takeda Shrine.","images":[{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c0/%E8%BA%91%E8%BA%85%E3%83%B6%E5%B4%8E%E9%A4%A8.jpg/960px-%E8%BA%91%E8%BA%85%E3%83%B6%E5%B4%8E%E9%A4%A8.jpg","caption":"Moat water and a vermilion bridge at the Tsutsujigasaki yakata site in Kofu, Yamanashi Prefecture. Wikimedia Commons."}],"sources":[{"label":"Wikipedia: Tsutsujigasaki Castle","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tsutsujigasaki_Castle"}]},
  {"name":"Matsumoto Castle","modern":"Matsumoto City, Nagano Prefecture","type":"hirajiro","clan":"Ogasawara / Ishikawa","lat":36.2391,"lon":137.9691194,"note":"Originally Fukashi Castle, contested by Ogasawara and Takeda in Shinano; its black-clad keep (built 1590s) is Japan's oldest surviving five-tier tenshu and a National Treasure, ringed by water moats.","images":[{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e3/Matsumoto_Castle_Keep_Tower.jpg/960px-Matsumoto_Castle_Keep_Tower.jpg","caption":"The black-lacquered keep of Matsumoto Castle rising from its stone base above the inner moat, Nagano Prefecture; Wikimedia Commons."}],"sources":[{"label":"Wikipedia: Matsumoto Castle","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Matsumoto_Castle"}]},
  {"name":"Ueda Castle","modern":"Ueda City, Nagano Prefecture","type":"hirajiro","clan":"Sanada","lat":36.4039222,"lon":138.2447944,"note":"Sanada Masayuki's fortress that twice repelled far larger Tokugawa armies (1585 and 1600), delaying Hidetada's force before Sekigahara, a celebrated feat of the Sanada clan.","images":[{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/13/Ueda_Castle_Amagafuchi.jpg/960px-Ueda_Castle_Amagafuchi.jpg","caption":"The reconstructed south and west turrets of Ueda Castle on their original stone bases above the Amagafuchi slope, Nagano Prefecture; Wikimedia Commons."}],"sources":[{"label":"Wikipedia: Ueda Castle","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ueda_Castle"}]},
  {"name":"Odani Castle","modern":"Nagahama City, Shiga Prefecture","type":"yamashiro","clan":"Azai","lat":35.45935917,"lon":136.2770722,"note":"Mountain seat of the Azai clan in northern Ōmi; fell to Nobunaga's 1573 siege, ending Azai Nagamasa (Nobunaga's brother-in-law) and his ally Asakura. One of Japan's five great mountain castles.","images":[{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/bc/Odanij18.jpg/960px-Odanij18.jpg","caption":"The Oishigaki, a surviving section of dry stone rampart in the ruins of Odani Castle, Nagahama, Shiga Prefecture; Wikimedia Commons."}],"sources":[{"label":"Wikipedia: Odani Castle","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Odani_Castle"}]},
  {"name":"Sawayama Castle","modern":"Hikone City, Shiga Prefecture","type":"yamashiro","clan":"Ishida","lat":35.2795,"lon":136.268925,"note":"Strategic mountain castle guarding the Ōmi corridor; held by Ishida Mitsunari, leader of the Western Army at Sekigahara (1600). Its functions were later moved to nearby Hikone Castle.","images":[{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/fb/The_Mt_Sawa_is_looked_at_from_Hikone_Castle_Shiga.jpg/960px-The_Mt_Sawa_is_looked_at_from_Hikone_Castle_Shiga.jpg","caption":"Mount Sawa, the wooded hill that carried Sawayama Castle, seen from the keep of Hikone Castle, Shiga Prefecture; Wikimedia Commons."}],"sources":[{"label":"Wikipedia: Sawayama Castle","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sawayama_Castle"}]},
  {"name":"Kuwana Castle","modern":"Kuwana City, Mie Prefecture","type":"hirajiro","clan":"Honda","lat":35.064625,"lon":136.698722,"note":"Coastal flatland castle at the mouth of the Ibi/Nagara rivers guarding the Tōkaidō crossing into Ise; major Tokugawa-era fortification developed by Honda Tadakatsu after 1601.","images":[{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f6/Kuwana_Castle_07.JPG/960px-Kuwana_Castle_07.JPG","caption":"The rebuilt Banryu-yagura turret at the site of Kuwana Castle, Mie Prefecture; Wikimedia Commons."}],"sources":[{"label":"Wikipedia: Kuwana Castle","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kuwana_Castle"}]},
  {"name":"Nagashino Castle","modern":"Shinshiro City, Aichi Prefecture","type":"hirajiro","clan":"Okudaira (Tokugawa)","lat":34.9228167,"lon":137.5598472,"note":"Small river-confluence fortress whose heroic defense by Okudaira Sadamasa triggered the 1575 Battle of Nagashino, where Oda–Tokugawa massed gunfire shattered the Takeda cavalry.","images":[{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/20/NagashinoC_Ushibuchibashi.jpg/960px-NagashinoC_Ushibuchibashi.jpg","caption":"The river confluence and cliffs below the site of Nagashino Castle, Shinshiro, Aichi Prefecture, seen from Ushibuchi Bridge; Wikimedia Commons."}],"sources":[{"label":"Wikipedia: Nagashino Castle","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Nagashino_Castle"}]},
  {"name":"Ichijōdani Castle (Asakura residence)","modern":"Fukui City, Fukui Prefecture","type":"yamashiro","clan":"Asakura","lat":35.9994917,"lon":136.2955639,"note":"Mountain fortress above the Asakura clan's castle town in Echizen, a flourishing cultural capital; Nobunaga destroyed it in 1573, and its excavated townscape is now a Special Historic Site.","images":[{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d1/Asakura_Yakata_of_Ichijodani_Asakura_Family_Historic_Ruins02s3s4440.jpg/960px-Asakura_Yakata_of_Ichijodani_Asakura_Family_Historic_Ruins02s3s4440.jpg","caption":"The Asakura clan residence compound (Asakura yakata) at the Ichijōdani ruins in Fukui, with its gate and earthen ramparts, Wikimedia Commons."}],"sources":[{"label":"Wikipedia: Ichijōdani Asakura Family Historic Ruins","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ichij%C5%8Ddani_Asakura_Family_Historic_Ruins"}]},
  {"name":"Takatō Castle","modern":"Ina City, Nagano Prefecture","type":"hirayamajiro","clan":"Takeda","lat":35.8330778,"lon":138.0628028,"note":"Key Takeda hilltop castle in southern Shinano; scene of the fierce 1582 last stand of Nishina Morinobu against Oda Nobutada during the Oda conquest of the Takeda. Famous today for its cherry blossoms.","images":[{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c4/TakatoCastle%EF%BC%88%E9%AB%98%E9%81%A0%E5%9F%8E%EF%BC%89.JPG/960px-TakatoCastle%EF%BC%88%E9%AB%98%E9%81%A0%E5%9F%8E%EF%BC%89.JPG","caption":"Cherry blossoms over the bridge and stone-faced moat of Takatō Castle Park in Ina, Nagano, Wikimedia Commons."}],"sources":[{"label":"Wikipedia: Takatō Castle","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Takat%C5%8D_Castle"}]},
  {"name":"Osaka Castle","modern":"Osaka, Osaka Prefecture","type":"hirajiro","clan":"Toyotomi (later Tokugawa)","lat":34.687378,"lon":135.525844,"note":"Toyotomi Hideyoshi's grand headquarters from 1583 and seat of his regime; site of the 1614-1615 Sieges of Osaka that ended the Toyotomi house and sealed Tokugawa supremacy.","images":[{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/ca/Osaka_Castle_03bs3200.jpg/960px-Osaka_Castle_03bs3200.jpg","caption":"The main keep of Osaka Castle rising above its Tokugawa-era stone ramparts and the modern city skyline, Wikimedia Commons."}],"sources":[{"label":"Wikipedia: Osaka Castle","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Osaka_Castle"}]},
  {"name":"Nijo Castle","modern":"Kyoto (Nakagyo Ward), Kyoto Prefecture","type":"hirajiro","clan":"Tokugawa shogunate","lat":35.014156,"lon":135.7475,"note":"Tokugawa Ieyasu's Kyoto residence and symbol of shogunal authority near the imperial court; in 1867 Tokugawa Yoshinobu announced the return of power to the Emperor here, ending the shogunate.","images":[{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/dd/NinomaruPalace.jpg/960px-NinomaruPalace.jpg","caption":"The gilded Karamon gate at Nijō Castle, with the Ninomaru Palace visible through it, Wikimedia Commons."}],"sources":[{"label":"Wikipedia: Nijō Castle","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Nij%C5%8D_Castle"}]},
  {"name":"Himeji Castle","modern":"Himeji, Hyogo Prefecture","type":"hirayamajiro","clan":"Ikeda (earlier Kuroda / Hashiba)","lat":34.839378,"lon":134.694097,"note":"Japan's finest surviving original castle and a UNESCO World Heritage Site; held in the Sengoku era by the Kuroda and Hashiba, then rebuilt into its iconic white form by Ikeda Terumasa after 1600.","images":[{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c1/Himeji_castle_in_may_2015.jpg/960px-Himeji_castle_in_may_2015.jpg","caption":"The white keep complex of Himeji Castle above its stone base, photographed in May 2015, Wikimedia Commons."}],"sources":[{"label":"Wikipedia: Himeji Castle","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Himeji_Castle"}]},
  {"name":"Bitchu-Matsuyama Castle","modern":"Takahashi, Okayama Prefecture","type":"yamashiro","clan":"Mimura (later Mizutani)","lat":34.809078,"lon":133.622303,"note":"The highest castle in Japan with an original keep (atop 430m Mt. Gagyu); a strategic Bitchu stronghold contested between the Mimura, Mori and Ukita during the Sengoku wars.","images":[{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/9e/Bitchu_Matsuyama_Castle_1.JPG/960px-Bitchu_Matsuyama_Castle_1.JPG","caption":"The honmaru of Bitchū Matsuyama Castle in Takahashi, Okayama, with its small keep above the stone ramparts, Wikimedia Commons."}],"sources":[{"label":"Wikipedia: Bitchū Matsuyama Castle","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bitch%C5%AB_Matsuyama_Castle"}]},
  {"name":"Tottori Castle","modern":"Tottori, Tottori Prefecture","type":"hirayamajiro","clan":"Yamana (later Ikeda)","lat":35.51,"lon":134.24111,"note":"Site of Hashiba (Toyotomi) Hideyoshi's notorious 1581 starvation siege ('Tottori no kaw-zeme'), one of the cruelest blockades of the Sengoku period. Hilltop fort on Mt. Kyusho.","images":[{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/8e/Tottori_castle04_2816.jpg/960px-Tottori_castle04_2816.jpg","caption":"The moat and stone ramparts at the foot of Mount Kyusho, site of Tottori Castle in Tottori Prefecture. Wikimedia Commons."}],"sources":[{"label":"Wikipedia: Tottori Castle","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tottori_Castle"}]},
  {"name":"Gassan-Toda Castle","modern":"Yasugi, Shimane Prefecture","type":"yamashiro","clan":"Amago","lat":35.36095,"lon":133.185261,"note":"Impregnable mountain seat of the Amago clan, who dominated the Izumo region; repelled a major Ōuchi siege in 1542-43 (led by Ōuchi Yoshitaka, with Mōri Motonari as an ally) before finally falling to Mori Motonari in 1566.","images":[{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/ab/%E6%9C%88%E5%B1%B1.jpg/960px-%E6%9C%88%E5%B1%B1.jpg","caption":"Mount Gassan at Yasugi, Shimane Prefecture, with the terraced enclosures of the Gassantoda Castle ruins along its ridge. Wikimedia Commons."}],"sources":[{"label":"Wikipedia: Gassantoda Castle","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gassantoda_Castle"}]},
  {"name":"Yoshida-Koriyama Castle","modern":"Akitakata, Hiroshima Prefecture","type":"yamashiro","clan":"Mori","lat":34.674286,"lon":132.709589,"note":"Sprawling mountain fortress (270+ enclosures) and birthplace of Mori power; Mori Motonari's 1540-41 defence here against a far larger Amago army launched his rise to mastery of western Honshu.","images":[{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/73/Yoshida-kouriyama_Castle_Honmaru.JPG/960px-Yoshida-kouriyama_Castle_Honmaru.JPG","caption":"The honmaru at the summit of the Yoshida-Koriyama Castle ruins, with the ninomaru in the foreground, Akitakata, Hiroshima Prefecture. Wikimedia Commons."}],"sources":[{"label":"Wikipedia: Yoshida-Kōriyama Castle","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Yoshida-K%C5%8Driyama_Castle"}]},
  {"name":"Yuzuki Castle","modern":"Matsuyama, Ehime Prefecture","type":"hirayamajiro","clan":"Kono","lat":33.848108,"lon":132.786742,"note":"Rare surviving moated low-hill castle on the Dogo plain; for two centuries the seat of the Kono clan who ruled Iyo Province until conquered by the Toyotomi-backed forces in 1585.","images":[{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/59/Yuzukijo_11.JPG/960px-Yuzukijo_11.JPG","caption":"A reconstructed samurai residence and earthen wall inside the Yuzuki Castle site, now Dogo Park in Matsuyama. Wikimedia Commons."}],"sources":[{"label":"Wikipedia: Yuzuki Castle","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Yuzuki_Castle"}]},
  {"name":"Urado Castle","modern":"Kochi, Kochi Prefecture","type":"hirayamajiro","clan":"Chosokabe","lat":33.497203,"lon":133.571986,"note":"Final headquarters of Chosokabe Motochika, unifier of Shikoku, after 1591; overlooks Tosa Bay. The Chosokabe were dispossessed here following their defeat at Sekigahara in 1600.","images":[{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5e/Urado_castle_02.jpg/960px-Urado_castle_02.jpg","caption":"A reconstructed section of stone walling at the Urado Castle site in Kochi. Wikimedia Commons."}],"sources":[{"label":"Japanese Wikipedia: Urado Castle (浦戸城)","url":"https://ja.wikipedia.org/wiki/浦戸城"}]},
  {"name":"Kumamoto Castle","modern":"Kumamoto (Chuo Ward), Kumamoto Prefecture","type":"hirayamajiro","clan":"Kato (later Hosokawa)","lat":32.806028,"lon":130.705897,"note":"Massive fortress built by master castle-builder Kato Kiyomasa from 1591, famed for its curved 'musha-gaeshi' stone walls; later withstood the 1877 Satsuma Rebellion siege.","images":[{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/ff/Kumamoto_Castle_Keep_Tower_20221022-3.jpg/960px-Kumamoto_Castle_Keep_Tower_20221022-3.jpg","caption":"The reconstructed great keep and small keep of Kumamoto Castle, photographed in October 2022. Wikimedia Commons."}],"sources":[{"label":"Wikipedia: Kumamoto Castle","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kumamoto_Castle"}]},
  {"name":"Funai Castle","modern":"Oita, Oita Prefecture","type":"mizujiro","clan":"Fukuhara / Takenaka / Matsudaira (former Ōtomo capital)","lat":33.240642,"lon":131.611433,"note":"Funai Castle stands on the coast at Ōita, on the site of the medieval provincial centre of Bungo. Construction began in 1597 under Fukuhara Naotaka, a vassal of Toyotomi Hideyoshi, and the castle was completed in 1599 as Niage Castle; Takenaka Shigetoshi, installed after Sekigahara, rebuilt it on a larger scale between 1601 and 1607. A great fire in 1743 destroyed most of the buildings including the three storey tenshu, which was never raised again, and the main gate with three turrets burned in the 1945 air raid on Ōita. Two Edo period turrets, the Hitojichi and Sōmon yagura, still stand together with stretches of stone wall, earthen wall and moat.","images":[{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/03/090505_Funai_Castle_Oita_Japan01bs.jpg/960px-090505_Funai_Castle_Oita_Japan01bs.jpg","caption":"Corner turret and white plastered wall on the stone ramparts of Funai Castle, Ōita city; Wikimedia Commons."}],"sources":[{"label":"Japanese Wikipedia: Funai Castle (府内城)","url":"https://ja.wikipedia.org/wiki/府内城"}]},
  {"name":"Kagoshima Castle (Tsurumaru)","modern":"Kagoshima, Kagoshima Prefecture","type":"hirayamajiro","clan":"Shimazu","lat":31.59825,"lon":130.554414,"note":"Kagoshima Castle, known locally as Tsurumaru, was begun by Shimazu Tadatsune after the Battle of Sekigahara and completed in 1604 as the seat of Satsuma. It was deliberately built in yakata style, more a large fortified residence than a fortress, with no tenshu and no high stone walls, a modest design intended as a political statement of Shimazu submission to the Tokugawa; Tadatsune's father Shimazu Yoshihiro objected that it was indefensible and too close to the sea. His misgivings were borne out during the Anglo-Satsuma War of 1863, although the British gunners mistook a nearby Buddhist temple for the castle tower and shelled that instead. The main citadel burned in 1873 and the second citadel during the Satsuma Rebellion of 1877; the site now holds the Reimeikan prefectural history centre, and the Goromon gate was reconstructed in 2020.","images":[{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/bd/Kagoshima_Castle_Goromon.jpg/960px-Kagoshima_Castle_Goromon.jpg","caption":"The Goromon, the great gate of Kagoshima (Tsurumaru) Castle, reconstructed in 2020; Wikimedia Commons."}],"sources":[{"label":"Wikipedia: Kagoshima Castle","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kagoshima_Castle"}]},
  {"name":"Tachibana-yama Castle","modern":"Fukuoka (Higashi Ward) / Shingu / Hisayama, Fukuoka Prefecture","type":"yamashiro","clan":"Tachibana (under Otomo)","lat":33.67973,"lon":130.468051,"note":"Tachibanayama Castle crowned the 367 metre Mount Tachibana north east of Hakata, a sprawling mountain fortress that used the whole massif of seven peaks and was known as the fortress of Chikuzen. It was raised in 1330 by Ōtomo Sadatoshi, second son of the Bungo shugo Ōtomo Sadamune, and his line took the Tachibana name from the place. Commanding the port of Hakata, it was fought over for decades by the Ōtomo, Ōuchi and Mōri, and in 1586 the young Tachibana Muneshige held it against a large Shimazu army that had just taken his birth father's Iwaya Castle. It was abandoned after Kuroda Nagamasa began Fukuoka Castle in 1601, much of its masonry being carried off for the new work, so that only traces of stone wall and an old well survive at the summit.","images":[{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/73/Stone_wall_of_Tachibana_Castle_Ruins.jpg/960px-Stone_wall_of_Tachibana_Castle_Ruins.jpg","caption":"Surviving dry stone rampart among the trees on Mount Tachibana, the site of Tachibanayama Castle; Wikimedia Commons."}],"sources":[{"label":"Wikipedia: Tachibanayama Castle","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tachibanayama_Castle"}]},
  {"name":"Saiki Castle","modern":"Saiki, Oita Prefecture","type":"yamashiro","clan":"Mori (Saiki branch)","lat":32.960231,"lon":131.889819,"note":"Saiki Castle was a yamajiro on the 144 metre Mount Yahata (Hachiman-yama), above the delta at the mouth of the Bansho River on the southern border of Bungo. Mōri Takamasa, relocated there by Tokugawa Ieyasu in 1601 with a holding of 20,000 koku, built it between 1602 and 1606 and laid out a new castle town below, replacing the remote Togamure Castle of the old Saeki clan. The design was overseen by Ichida Yusada, formerly involved in the building of Azuchi, and the stone walls by a master who had worked on those of Himeji; the three storey tenshu was lost to fire only about fifteen years after completion. Because the mountain site was inconvenient in peacetime, a sannomaru was built at the foot of the hill in 1637 for the daimyō residence and domain offices, and the Mōri ruled Saiki for twelve generations until the buildings were sold off or destroyed in 1871.","images":[{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e5/Saiki_castle_05.jpg/960px-Saiki_castle_05.jpg","caption":"Stone walls of the hilltop enclosures of Saiki Castle on Mount Shiroyama, Ōita Prefecture; Wikimedia Commons."}],"sources":[{"label":"Wikipedia: Saiki Castle","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Saiki_Castle"}]},
  {"name":"Oka Castle","modern":"Taketa, Oita Prefecture","type":"yamashiro","clan":"Shiga (under Otomo; later Nakagawa)","lat":32.969217,"lon":131.408069,"note":"Oka Castle ran for over a kilometre along the ridge of Tenjinyama above Taketa in south western Bungo, flanked by the Inaba and Tamaki rivers and commanding the roads that cross central Kyushu. Legend dates its origin to 1185 and Shiga Sadatomo is said to have enlarged and renamed it in 1334, though the early record is uncertain. Its most celebrated moment came in 1586, when Shiga Chikatsugu and some 2,000 men held the castle against a Shimazu army numbering over 30,000 until Toyotomi Hideyoshi intervened; from 1594 the Nakagawa clan held it for thirteen generations without transfer or reduction. The tenshu collapsed in an earthquake in 1769, fire destroyed much of the rest in 1771, and the surviving structures were demolished by 1873, leaving the towering stone ramparts that inspired Taki Rentarō's Kōjō no Tsuki.","images":[{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/3b/Okajoshi.jpg/960px-Okajoshi.jpg","caption":"The high stone ramparts of Oka Castle along the clifftop ridge at Taketa, Ōita Prefecture; Wikimedia Commons."}],"sources":[{"label":"Wikipedia: Oka Castle","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Oka_Castle"}]},
  // ---- Added 2026-08-01. Closes two audited gaps: 8 of Japan's 12 surviving original
  // keeps were absent (2 of them National Treasures), and 24 castles had a siege pinned
  // but no castle. Triage kept 7 of those 24; the rest were one-battle outworks.
  // Every coordinate cross-checked against Wikipedia or Wikidata; images HEAD-verified. ----
  {"name":"Kanegasaki Castle","modern":"Tsuruga, Fukui Prefecture","type":"yamashiro","clan":"Nitta (Southern Court), later Asakura","lat":35.666083,"lon":136.074167,"note":"Kanegasaki sits on a wooded ridge about 86 metres high that drops straight into Tsuruga Bay, guarding the harbour where the routes north from Lake Biwa reach the Japan Sea. A fort was first raised here by Taira no Michimori during the Genpei War of the 1180s, against Kiso Yoshinaka; the ridge was refortified by later lords of Echizen and in the Sengoku period belonged to the Asakura.\n\nTwo famous episodes belong to it. In 1337 Nitta Yoshisada held Kanegasaki for the Southern Court, sheltering two sons of Emperor Go-Daigo. Ashikaga forces besieged it for three months; Yoshisada slipped away to Somayama to raise a relief force, but the counter-attack failed and the garrison was reduced to eating its horses. When Kō no Moroyasu broke through in the spring of 1337, Prince Takanaga and Yoshisada's son Nitta Yoshiaki took their own lives along with some three hundred followers. Prince Tsunenaga escaped, only to be caught and killed. It was the end of Nitta resistance in the Hokuriku.\n\nIn 1570 Oda Nobunaga invaded Echizen to punish Asakura Yoshikage, stormed the neighbouring hill of Tezutsuyama and took Kanegasaki the next day, the assault led by Kinoshita Tōkichirō, the future Toyotomi Hideyoshi. Word then came that Nobunaga's brother-in-law Azai Nagamasa had changed sides and was closing the road home. Nobunaga bolted for Kyoto while Hideyoshi, with Akechi Mitsuhide, commanded the rearguard that held the pass behind him. The retreat from Kanegasaki became one of the best known feats of Hideyoshi's career, and Nobunaga's revenge followed at Anegawa that summer and at Odani in 1573.\n\nNothing is built on the hill today. Visitors find terraced enclosures, dry moats cut across the ridge and the footings of the main hall and gate; the site has been a National Historic Site since 1934. At the foot of the slope stands Kanegasaki-gū, a shrine founded in 1890 by the Meiji government to commemorate the princes who died here. The castle was also known as Tsuruga Castle after its town, which is a different place from the Tsuruga Castle of Aizu-Wakamatsu.","images":[{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/9c/Kanegasaki_Castle_Site_View_from_Tezutsu-yama.jpg/960px-Kanegasaki_Castle_Site_View_from_Tezutsu-yama.jpg","caption":"The wooded headland of the Kanegasaki Castle site above Tsuruga harbour in Fukui Prefecture, seen from Mount Tezutsu; only earthworks survive, with no keep or other buildings, Wikimedia Commons."}],"sources":[{"label":"Wikipedia: Kanagasaki Castle","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kanagasaki_Castle"}]},
  {"name":"Chihaya Castle","modern":"Chihayaakasaka, Osaka Prefecture","type":"yamashiro","clan":"Kusunoki","lat":34.417225,"lon":135.651197,"note":"Chihaya was thrown up around 1332 by Kusunoki Masashige, a landholder of the Chihaya-Akasaka district of Kawachi, on a ridge running down from Mount Kongō at the Yamato border, roughly five kilometres above his main base at Akasaka. It was a pure mountain fortress with no stone walls and no keep. Five enclosures were strung along about 200 metres of ridge and separated from one another by dry moats, defended by timber palisades, earth banks, removable bridges and cliffs falling away to rivers on both sides. The only approach was a climb of some five hundred steep, narrow steps. The main enclosure measured about 100 by 20 metres and may have carried a single wooden tower, yet the place could hold several hundred men and, decisively, had its own well.\n\nIn 1333 the Kamakura shogunate sent a large army against Kusunoki after he emerged from hiding and struck at shogunal positions in Kii, Osaka and Kyoto. The attackers took nearby Kami-Akasaka in a fortnight, then stalled at Chihaya for months. Assaults failed on the slope, the valley was too cramped for proper siege works, and mountain ascetics loyal to Prince Moriyoshi carried supplies in over Mount Kongō. The chronicle Taiheiki credits Kusunoki with constant raids, felled trees and rolled boulders, and straw dummies that drew wasted volleys. The siege tied down shogunal strength and cost it enormous prestige while Emperor Go-Daigo escaped from Oki and Ashikaga Takauji changed sides; Kamakura fell the same year. Chihaya then served as the Kusunoki family seat until 1392, when Hatakeyama Motokuni of the Northern Court defeated Kusunoki Masakatsu and the castle was abandoned.\n\nNothing built stands today. The ruins have been a National Historic Site since 1934 and the site was named one of Japan's Top 100 Castles in 2006. What survives is the earthwork itself: the terraced enclosures along the ridge, the dry moats cut between them and the long stone stairway. A Meiji period shrine to Masashige, Chihaya Jinja, occupies the second bailey, with its offices in the third.","images":[{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/dd/Scale_model_of_Chihaya_castle.jpg/960px-Scale_model_of_Chihaya_castle.jpg","caption":"A modern scale model of Chihaya Castle at Chihayaakasaka, Osaka, showing the timber palisades and the enclosures strung along the ridge; nothing of the fortress itself stands on the site today, Wikimedia Commons."}],"sources":[{"label":"Wikipedia: Chihaya Castle","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Chihaya_Castle"}]},
  {"name":"Nanao Castle","modern":"Nanao, Ishikawa Prefecture","type":"yamashiro","clan":"Hatakeyama (Noto branch); later Uesugi, then Maeda","lat":37.0089,"lon":136.9841,"note":"Nanao sits on a ridge about 300 metres above Nanao Bay at the north end of the Sekidō range, and the name comes from the seven spurs that branch off it. The Hatakeyama began fortifying the ridge early in the fifteenth century, after Hatakeyama Mitsunori was made shugo (military governor) of Noto in 1408, and the family ruled the province from this mountain for roughly 180 years. Hatakeyama Yoshifusa (1491 to 1545) turned a hill fort into one of the five largest mountain castles of medieval Japan, running some 2.5 km north to south and 1 km east to west. The plan was a ladder of terraced baileys: the lord's enclosure stood at the summit and could be reached only by climbing through the walled compounds of the senior retainers, the Yusa and the Chō, with lesser vassals below them and a fortified castle town on the flat ground at the foot. Uesugi Kenshin invaded Noto in 1576, reduced the outlying forts, and could not take the mountain. He returned in 1577 and Nanao held out for about a year, until its lord Hatakeyama Yoshitaka died, of epidemic or of poison depending on the account, leaving a child as nominal head while the chief retainers quarrelled. Yusa Tsugumitsu opened the gates to the Uesugi in the ninth month of 1577, and the Chō, who wanted to fight on, were killed. Kenshin climbed to the summit bailey and wrote to his retainers about the view, then destroyed the Oda relief army under Shibata Katsuie at the Tedori River. Oda forces took Noto in 1581 and Maeda Toshiie received the province; he preferred a site down by the harbour, and Nanao was abandoned in 1589. Because nothing was ever built over it, Nanao is among the best preserved mountain castle sites in Japan. Rough piled stone walls survive around almost every bailey, most strikingly the five stepped tiers at the main enclosure and at the Sakura no baba parade ground. The ruins have been a National Historic Site since 1934, and there is a castle museum at the foot of the climb. The Noto earthquake of 1 January 2024 brought down parts of the walls at the main and second enclosures and at the Sakura no baba.","images":[{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/9a/Nanaojo-tyoudomaru.JPG/960px-Nanaojo-tyoudomaru.JPG","caption":"Terraced dry stone ramparts of the Chōdomaru bailey at the Nanao Castle ruins in Nanao, Ishikawa Prefecture, Wikimedia Commons."}],"sources":[{"label":"Wikipedia: Nanao Castle","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Nanao_Castle"}]},
  {"name":"Miki Castle","modern":"Miki, Hyōgo Prefecture","type":"hirayamajiro","clan":"Bessho","lat":34.7999,"lon":134.988,"note":"Miki stood on a terrace above the Mino River, on the road between Kyoto and the Arima hot springs, and Japanese tradition counts it with Gochaku and Aga as one of the three great castles of Harima. It was raised around 1492 by Bessho Noriharu, an Akamatsu deputy rewarded with eight districts after he helped restore his lord to the governorship, and it stayed the seat of his line for some ninety years. His grandson Bessho Nariharu was driven out briefly by the Uragami in 1530, held off two Amago invasions in 1538 and 1539, and bent to the Miyoshi around 1550 before recovering his independence; the castle was much enlarged in those years. Cliffs, valleys and the river closed it on nearly every side, leaving only the south east open to attack.\n\nIn 1578 Noriharu's descendant Bessho Nagaharu broke with Oda Nobunaga just as Hashiba Hideyoshi opened the western campaign. Hideyoshi answered with a blockade rather than an assault, ringing Miki with siege forts and earth walls and starving it for roughly twenty months, a campaign remembered as the starving out of Miki. The defeat of the Mōri fleet at Kizugawa in late 1578 and the Ukita defection in 1579 shut the last routes in. Nagaharu surrendered in January 1580 and took his own life on terms that spared his garrison.\n\nHideyoshi's officers held Miki as a local strongpoint until it was wound up in the mid 1610s under the Tokugawa rule of one castle per province. The main bailey is now Uenomaru Park, with the city history museum beside it, and baileys, earthen banks, dry ditches, wells and the platform of the keep can still be traced, along with a stele carrying Nagaharu's farewell poem. In 2013 the castle site was designated a National Historic Site together with the ring of Hideyoshi's siege forts and ramparts around it.","images":[{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/fa/Mikij10.jpg/960px-Mikij10.jpg","caption":"A modern ornamental wall raised on the site of Miki Castle in Uenomaru Park, Miki, Hyōgo; no original castle buildings survive, Wikimedia Commons."}],"sources":[{"label":"Wikipedia: Miki Castle","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Miki_Castle"}]},
  {"name":"Takatenjin Castle","modern":"Kakegawa, Shizuoka Prefecture","type":"yamashiro","clan":"Ogasawara (for Imagawa, then Tokugawa), later Takeda","lat":34.6985,"lon":138.0353,"note":"Takatenjin stood on Mount Kakuo, a steep hill of 132 metres in Totomi rising about 100 metres above the surrounding country, climbable only by narrow paths. Stories of a twelfth century fort are unproven; excavated pottery suggests a stronghold here by the late fifteenth century, and the first firm record is of Kushima Sukeharu holding it for the Imagawa in the early sixteenth. After the Kushima fell in the Hanakura succession war of 1536 the Imagawa passed it to the Ogasawara, who went over to Tokugawa Ieyasu when the Imagawa collapsed. Contemporaries called it the key to Totomi, commanding the roads to the coast and the Tokaido eleven kilometres south.\n\nTakeda Shingen besieged it in 1571 and failed. His son Katsuyori stormed it in 1574, carrying the second bailey and forcing the surrender of Ogasawara Ujisuke, also recorded as Nagatada; taking what his father could not made Katsuyori's name. He then extended the castle west along the ridge, where Takatenjin Shrine now stands, and installed Okabe Motonobu as governor. Ieyasu answered with patience. He seized Suwahara Castle to cut the supply road from the Oi River, built Yokosuka Castle and six siege forts around the mountain, and from 1580 simply starved the garrison out; a letter from Nobunaga instructing him to refuse any surrender survives. Late in the third month of 1581 Okabe led his remaining men out in a hopeless charge and was killed. Katsuyori's failure to relieve the place destroyed his standing with his own vassals, and the Takeda were finished the next year.\n\nTakatenjin was abandoned after 1581 and never refortified, which is why so much of it survives. There is no stone walling. The baileys, earthen ramparts and cut ditches of a late Takeda mountain castle lie almost undisturbed under the trees, together with the ridge path by which the inspector Yokota Tadatoshi escaped to carry news of the fall to Katsuyori. The ruins are a National Historic Site, designated in 1975 and extended in 2007, and number 147 of the Continued Top 100 Castles. A mock keep put up before the Second World War burned after a lightning strike, leaving only its concrete footing; Takatenjin Shrine stands on the summit.","images":[{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/8e/Sodekuruwa_Ato_201802late.jpg/960px-Sodekuruwa_Ato_201802late.jpg","caption":"Earthen ramparts of the sode-kuruwa outwork at the Takatenjin Castle site in Kakegawa, Shizuoka Prefecture, Wikimedia Commons."}],"sources":[{"label":"Wikipedia: Takatenjin Castle","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Takatenjin_Castle"}]},
  {"name":"Tanabe Castle (Tango)","modern":"Maizuru, Kyoto Prefecture","type":"hirajiro","clan":"Hosokawa, then Kyōgoku and Makino","lat":35.4457806,"lon":135.330975,"note":"Hosokawa Fujitaka, better known by his Buddhist name Yūsai, built Tanabe in 1582 on the site of an earlier Isshiki fortification, on flat ground hemmed in by the Isatsu and Takano rivers, with Maizuru Bay to the north and marsh to the south. The cramped site forced an unusually compact plan, the baileys wrapped in rings around the centre rather than spread along a ridge. In 1600 Fujitaka held it with about 500 men while his son Tadaoki rode east with Tokugawa Ieyasu, and roughly 15,000 troops of the western coalition closed in. The siege ran some fifty days and was never pressed hard; many of the besiegers had studied poetry under Fujitaka and are said to have fired their cannon without shot. He was by then the last living holder of the Kokin denju, the secret transmission of how to read the tenth century Kokin Wakashū anthology, and rather than let it die with him he sent the certificates out of the castle to Prince Hachijō Toshihito. The court, alarmed that the tradition would be lost, dispatched imperial envoys with a decree ordering the fighting stopped, and Fujitaka gave up the castle nineteen days before Sekigahara, having tied down a substantial western force for the whole campaign. Tanabe then became a domain seat, held by the Kyōgoku from 1621 and the Makino from 1668 until the Meiji Restoration; the domain was renamed Maizuru in 1869 to end the confusion with Tanabe in Kii Province. The buildings were cleared in the 1870s and every moat has since been filled in. The site is now Maizuru Park, where stretches of stone wall, the stone platform of the keep and a garden survive, along with a two storey corner turret rebuilt in 1940 as the Shōkokan and a gate reconstructed in 1992 that houses the castle museum.","images":[{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/cb/Maizuru_city_Tanabe_Castle_ac_%281%29.jpg/960px-Maizuru_city_Tanabe_Castle_ac_%281%29.jpg","caption":"The Shōkokan, a two storey corner turret rebuilt in 1940 on surviving stonework at the Tanabe Castle site in Maizuru Park, Kyoto Prefecture, and a modern reconstruction rather than an original building, Wikimedia Commons."}],"sources":[{"label":"Wikipedia: Tanabe Castle (Tango)","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tanabe_Castle_(Tango)"}]},
  {"name":"Yanagawa Castle","modern":"Yanagawa, Fukuoka Prefecture","type":"mizujiro","clan":"Kamachi, then Tachibana (Tanaka 1600 to 1620)","lat":33.16072,"lon":130.40083,"note":"Yanagawa stands on the Chikugo delta a few kilometres from the Ariake Sea, on ground so flat that its whole defence was water. Kamachi Haruhisa built it between 1501 and 1504 as an outwork of Kamachi Castle and then moved his house into it; his grandson Kamachi Akimori rebuilt it in earnest in the 1560s, laying out the lattice of channels that made it the hardest place in Kyushu to take. A jingle sung in the Ōtomo camp allowed three months for Higo and a morning for Hizen and Chikuzen, but three years for Yanagawa. Ryūzōji Takanobu and Nabeshima Naoshige could not storm it with twenty thousand men, and neither could the Ōtomo generals Tachibana Dōsetsu and Takahashi Jōun. The Kamachi were destroyed by treachery instead in 1581, and Ryūzōji retainers moved into the castle they had failed to carry. After Hideyoshi's Kyushu campaign of 1587 it went to Dōsetsu's adopted son Tachibana Muneshige at 132,000 koku. Muneshige backed the losing Western army in 1600, was besieged here by Katō Kiyomasa, Kuroda Yoshitaka and Nabeshima Naoshige, and gave the castle up on terms. Tanaka Yoshimasa then held all Chikugo at 325,000 koku and enlarged the works to match; when his line died out without an heir in 1620, Muneshige was brought back at 109,000 koku, the only lord stripped of his domain after Sekigahara who ever recovered it. The Tachibana held Yanagawa until 1871. Fire gutted the castle in February 1872, possibly set to keep the family's treasures from the new government, and the five storey keep, known from a photograph taken about 1870, was never rebuilt; in 1874 its stone was carted away to patch a breached sea dyke. The honmaru, a city historic site, is now school ground, the keep platform left as a grassy hummock the pupils run up for training. What really survives is the water. Some 470 km of canals still thread the town and are worked by flat punts, and Ohana, the Tachibana villa begun west of the castle in 1697, keeps its Shōtō-en garden.","images":[{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/87/Yanagawa_Castle.jpg/960px-Yanagawa_Castle.jpg","caption":"Yanagawa Castle in Chikugo, now Fukuoka Prefecture, photographed about 1870, showing the five storey keep that burned in 1872 and was never rebuilt; Wikimedia Commons."}],"sources":[{"label":"Japanese Wikipedia: Yanagawa Castle (柳川城)","url":"https://ja.wikipedia.org/wiki/柳川城"}]},
  {"name":"Hikone Castle","modern":"Hikone, Shiga Prefecture","type":"hirayamajiro","clan":"Ii","lat":35.27639,"lon":136.25167,"note":"Hikone was begun by Ii Naomasa, one of Tokugawa Ieyasu's most trusted generals, who was granted eastern Omi after Sekigahara (1600) together with Ishida Mitsunari's former seat at Sawayama. Naomasa disliked Sawayama; it was an old-fashioned hill fort split awkwardly between summit and slope, and it carried the taint of the defeated enemy. He chose instead a low hill, Mount Konki, then standing directly on the shore of Lake Biwa, where the Nakasendo highway squeezed through a natural bottleneck between lake and mountains, the corridor any army marching on Kyoto from the east would have to use. Naomasa died in 1602 of wounds taken at Sekigahara and his son Ii Naokatsu completed the castle in 1622. To save time and money the Ii pulled down the other fortresses in their territory and carted the pieces here: the three-storey keep came from Otsu Castle, the Tenbin turret from Nagahama, the Nishinomaru three-storey turret from Odani.\n\nFor the next two and a half centuries Hikone was the seat of the Ii, reckoned the foremost of the fudai daimyo, the hereditary Tokugawa vassals. Its best known lord was Ii Naosuke, who as tairo effectively governed Japan in the 1850s, signed the treaties that ended the country's seclusion, purged his opponents, and was cut down by Mito ronin outside Edo Castle's Sakurada Gate in 1860.\n\nWhen the Meiji government ordered feudal fortifications demolished, Hikone was spared after the Emperor Meiji, touring the region, asked directly that it be kept. It therefore survives more completely than almost any other Japanese castle. The keep with its attached turrets is a National Treasure, one of only five so designated and one of the twelve original keeps still standing; the stables, the Tenbin turret, the Drum Gate and two further turret complexes are Important Cultural Properties. The daimyo palace was rebuilt in 1987 as the Hikone Castle Museum. Land reclamation has since left the castle roughly a kilometre back from the lake it once guarded.","images":[{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/9f/Hikone_Castle_November_2016_-02.jpg/960px-Hikone_Castle_November_2016_-02.jpg","caption":"The National Treasure keep of Hikone Castle on Mount Konki, Hikone, Shiga Prefecture, an original Edo-period tower rather than a modern reconstruction. Wikimedia Commons."}],"sources":[{"label":"Wikipedia: Hikone Castle","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hikone_Castle"}]},
  {"name":"Matsue Castle","modern":"Matsue, Shimane Prefecture","type":"hirayamajiro","clan":"Horio, then Kyōgoku, then Matsudaira","lat":35.474977,"lon":133.050556,"note":"Matsue Castle was built between 1607 and 1611 by Horio Yoshiharu, who had been granted Izumo and Oki with 240,000 koku after Sekigahara. He inherited the Amago clan's old mountain fastness at Gassan-Toda, judged it impractical for governing in peacetime, and moved his seat to Kameda, a hill of only 29 metres on the low ground between Lake Shinji and the Nakaumi lagoon, laying out a new castle town around it. The design is credited to Oze Hoan. Yoshiharu died in 1611, the year the work was finished. The Horio line failed in 1633 and the Kyogoku held Matsue briefly; from 1638 Matsudaira Naomasa, a grandson of Tokugawa Ieyasu transferred in from Matsumoto, began a tenure of ten generations that lasted until the Meiji Restoration. No siege was ever fought here. The castle was raised after the fighting had stopped, and its interest lies in being the administrative capital of Izumo rather than a battlefield.\n\nThe keep is the only original one surviving anywhere in the San'in region. It is a watchtower type tower roughly 30 metres tall, dark boarded, showing five roofs outside but six floors within, and it is one of just five keeps designated National Treasures, with Himeji, Matsumoto, Inuyama and Hikone. Under the Meiji abolition of castles the site went to the army and every building was to be sold off for demolition; local supporters bought the keep back and saved it, along with one attached turret and the stone walls. Prayer plaques dated to 1611 were found at Matsue Shrine in 2012 and fixed the construction date, which cleared the way for National Treasure status in July 2015. Several second bailey turrets were rebuilt in 2001 and the grounds are now a public park.","images":[{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/74/080720_Matsue_Castle_Matsue_Shimane_pref_Japan01s.jpg/960px-080720_Matsue_Castle_Matsue_Shimane_pref_Japan01s.jpg","caption":"The keep of Matsue Castle in Matsue, Shimane Prefecture, photographed in 2008; this is the original 1611 wooden tower, not a modern reconstruction. Wikimedia Commons."}],"sources":[{"label":"Wikipedia: Matsue Castle","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Matsue_Castle"}]},
  {"name":"Maruoka Castle","modern":"Sakai, Fukui Prefecture","type":"hirayamajiro","clan":"Shibata, then Honda and Arima","lat":36.152347,"lon":136.272097,"note":"Maruoka stands on a low isolated hill in the Sakai plain north of Fukui, where the Hokurikudō met the road running inland to Mino. Oda Nobunaga crushed the Ikkō ikki rising in Echizen in 1575 and handed the province to Shibata Katsuie, whose nephew and adopted son Shibata Katsutoyo is traditionally said to have come down from Toyohara and raised this castle in 1576. Katsutoyo was moved to Nagahama after Nobunaga's death and died in 1583, the year Hideyoshi destroyed Katsuie at Kitanoshō. The Aoyama held Maruoka next, backed the losing Western army at Sekigahara in 1600 and were stripped of it. Ieyasu gave Echizen to his son Yūki Hideyasu, and from 1612 the castle went to Honda Narishige, at first as a guardian attached to Fukui and from the 1620s as daimyō of Maruoka in his own right; his heirs finished the castle and its town. The Honda were dispossessed after a succession quarrel in 1695, and the Arima held the domain until the han were abolished in 1871.\n\nThe small three storey keep is one of only twelve original keeps left in Japan and the only one in the Hokuriku region. It looks archaic, a watchtower with an open railed gallery set on a broad hipped roof, and was long advertised as the oldest keep in the country. That claim has collapsed. A survey commissioned by Sakai City, using tree ring dating and isotope analysis, reported in March 2019 that its main structural pillars come from timber felled after 1626, so the tower now standing is almost certainly a Honda rebuilding of the Kan'ei era, most likely 1628. Its roof tiles are cut from local shakudani stone rather than fired clay, a Hokuriku answer to snow and frost, which makes the roof extremely heavy. The keep fell down completely in the Fukui earthquake of 1948 when its base of rough uncut stone gave way, and was re-erected in 1955 around a concrete frame reusing most of its own fallen timbers. It is an Important Cultural Property, having been a National Treasure under the prewar law. The grounds are now Kasumigajō Park, planted with some 400 cherry trees; the pentagonal inner moat was filled in between late Taishō and early Shōwa, and two castle gates survive at temples in Awara and Komatsu.","images":[{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d1/Maruoka_Castle_20100529-01.jpg/960px-Maruoka_Castle_20100529-01.jpg","caption":"The original keep of Maruoka Castle in Sakai, Fukui Prefecture, on its base of rough uncut stone; it is not a modern replica, but it collapsed in the 1948 Fukui earthquake and was re-erected in 1955 from its own salvaged timbers, Wikimedia Commons."}],"sources":[{"label":"Wikipedia: Maruoka Castle","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Maruoka_Castle"}]},
  {"name":"Kōchi Castle","modern":"Kōchi, Kōchi Prefecture","type":"hirayamajiro","clan":"Yamauchi (earlier Chōsokabe)","lat":33.561111,"lon":133.531389,"note":"Kōchi Castle stands on Ōtakasaka, a low hill in the middle of the Kōchi plain where two rivers meet. Chōsokabe Motochika began building here in the late 1580s, but the ground flooded and he moved his headquarters to Urado on the coast instead. After Sekigahara the Chōsokabe lost Tosa, and in 1601 the Tokugawa handed the province to Yamauchi Kazutoyo, a Kakegawa lord rewarded for backing Ieyasu. Kazutoyo judged Urado too cramped and too weak to hold a whole province, went back to the hill Motochika had given up on, and diked and drained the site. Work ran from 1601 to about 1611. The castle was first called Kawanakayama, the mountain among the rivers, and was renamed Kōchi soon after. His heirs governed Tosa from it until the domains were abolished in 1871, and the bad blood between the incoming Yamauchi retainers and the dispossessed Chōsokabe warriors shaped Tosa politics for the rest of the Edo period. A fire in 1727 burned almost the whole castle down. It was rebuilt between 1729 and 1753 to the old design rather than to a newer fashion, which is why the place still looks like a castle of about 1600. Kōchi then came through the Meiji demolitions and the wartime bombing of the city better than almost anywhere else in Japan. Its keep is one of only twelve that were never pulled down and replaced, and Kōchi is the only castle in the country that still has its keep and its honmaru palace, the Kaitokukan, standing together, with the gates, walls and storehouses of the inner enceinte around them. Fifteen structures are Important Cultural Properties and the grounds are a National Historic Site.","images":[{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/94/Kochi_Castle%2C_enkei.jpg/960px-Kochi_Castle%2C_enkei.jpg","caption":"Kōchi Castle in Kōchi Prefecture seen from a distance, its Edo period keep and honmaru buildings, rebuilt after the 1727 fire and never demolished since, standing above the city. Wikimedia Commons."}],"sources":[{"label":"Wikipedia: Kōchi Castle","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/K%C5%8Dchi_Castle"}]},
  {"name":"Uwajima Castle","modern":"Uwajima, Ehime Prefecture","type":"hirayamajiro","clan":"Todo, then Date (Uwajima branch)","lat":33.2194778,"lon":132.5652361,"note":"The hill above Uwajima Bay was fortified as early as the Heian period, against the pirate rebellion of Fujiwara no Sumitomo, and a branch of the Saionji family held the site through the Muromachi period until Chōsokabe Motochika overran southern Iyo. The castle standing today is essentially the work of Tōdō Takatora, the most inventive castle builder of his generation, who was given the district by Toyotomi Hideyoshi and rebuilt it between 1596 and about 1601. Takatora used an irregular five sided plan instead of the usual concentric rectangles, so that an attacker who worked out the line of one wall found the next one running away at an unexpected angle, and the sea originally came up to the eastern foot of the hill. After Takatora transferred to Imabari the castle passed briefly to Tomita Nobutaka, then in 1614 to Date Hidemune, eldest son of Date Masamune of Sendai. His line held Uwajima as a branch domain rated at a hundred thousand koku, reduced when a sub fief was split off at Iyo-Yoshida in 1657, and it produced Date Munenari, one of the reforming lords who shaped national politics in the 1860s. Earthquake damage in 1649 forced a long rebuilding, and in 1666 Hidemune's son Munetoshi put up the three storey keep that still stands. Uwajima never fell to an assault; its losses came in peacetime. Most of the outer walls, gates and turrets were cleared between 1900 and 1913 to enlarge the harbour, and the Ōtemon gate, made a National Treasure alongside the keep in 1934, burned in an American air raid in the closing weeks of the Pacific War. The Date family handed the keep and its grounds to the city in 1949. What survives is the original wooden keep, one of only twelve in Japan never rebuilt and now an Important Cultural Property, the Noboritachimon on the southern path up the hill, a rare large gate of its type protected as a city cultural property, and the stone walls of the inner bailey. The whole hill has been a National Historic Site since 1937.","images":[{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f9/Uwajima_Castle_Keep_Tower_20170611.jpg/960px-Uwajima_Castle_Keep_Tower_20170611.jpg","caption":"The three storey keep of Uwajima Castle in Ehime Prefecture, built in 1666 and one of Japan's twelve surviving original keeps rather than a modern reconstruction, Wikimedia Commons."}],"sources":[{"label":"Wikipedia: Uwajima Castle","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Uwajima_Castle"}]},
  {"name":"Marugame Castle","modern":"Marugame, Kagawa Prefecture","type":"hirayamajiro","clan":"Ikoma, Yamazaki, then Kyogoku","lat":34.286286,"lon":133.800097,"note":"Marugame stands on Kameyama, a low hill in western Sanuki near the Inland Sea coast of Shikoku. Toyotomi Hideyoshi granted the whole province to Ikoma Chikamasa in 1587; Chikamasa settled on Takamatsu as his seat and began a second castle here in the 1590s to hold western Sanuki, then passed it to his son Kazumasa. The Ikoma hedged their way through Sekigahara in 1600, the father backing the Western army and the son the Eastern, and kept the province. What they could not survive was the peace: the shogunate's one province, one castle edict of 1615 left Takamatsu standing and Marugame condemned. The site was revived in 1640, when the Ikoma were stripped of Sanuki after an internal revolt and the western districts went to Yamazaki Ieharu, a lord with a reputation for fortification who had worked on Osaka and Shimabara. He rebuilt Marugame on a scale well beyond what his small domain warranted. His line failed in 1658 and a cadet branch of the Kyogoku clan came in from Tatsuno in Harima; they raised the present keep in 1660, saw the works finished by 1670, and held Marugame Domain to the end of the Edo period.\n\nThe castle is remembered for its masonry rather than its buildings. Four tiers of wall climb roughly sixty metres from the foot of the hill to the top enclosure, the greatest stacked height of any castle in Japan, and each face swells outward at the base in the fan shaped curve masons called ogi no kobai. It is the clearest teaching example of how a hilltop was engineered rather than merely occupied. The three storey keep of 1660 survives, at about fifteen metres the smallest of Japan's twelve original keeps, along with the two gates of the main entrance; all are Important Cultural Properties, and the site has been a National Historic Site since 1953. Fire in 1869 destroyed the daimyo palace, the moats were filled from 1870, and army demolitions in 1876 took the turrets and much outer walling, but the keep and central baileys were spared and opened as Kameyama Park in 1919. A stretch of the outer stonework collapsed after the heavy rains and typhoon of 2018 and is being dismantled and relaid stone by stone, work expected to run into the late 2020s.","images":[{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/56/Marugame_Castle01.jpg/960px-Marugame_Castle01.jpg","caption":"Marugame Castle in Kagawa Prefecture, its tiered stone walls climbing Kameyama hill to the small original keep of 1660; Wikimedia Commons."}],"sources":[{"label":"Wikipedia: Marugame Castle","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Marugame_Castle"}]},
  {"name":"Matsuyama Castle (Iyo)","modern":"Matsuyama, Ehime Prefecture","type":"hirayamajiro","clan":"Kato, then Gamo, then Hisamatsu-Matsudaira","lat":33.845539,"lon":132.765722,"note":"Matsuyama Castle stands on Mount Katsuyama, a 132 metre hill in the middle of the modern city. It was begun in 1602 by Kato Yoshiaki, who had made his name fighting for Toyotomi Hideyoshi at Shizugatake and then joined Tokugawa Ieyasu at Sekigahara, and was rewarded with the Iyo-Matsuyama domain of 200,000 koku. Yoshiaki moved his seat away from the old Kono country around Yuzuki and laid out a new hilltop fortress with a planned castle town beneath it. The work took a quarter of a century. Yoshiaki was transferred to Aizu just before completion in 1627, and it fell to Gamo Tadatomo to finish a five storey keep; Tadatomo died in 1634 without an heir, and the shogunate installed Matsudaira Sadayuki of the Hisamatsu-Matsudaira, a house reckoned kin to the Tokugawa, who held Matsuyama until 1868. Sadayuki rebuilt the keep smaller, at three storeys, in 1642. Lightning burned it in 1784, and the twelfth daimyo, Matsudaira Katsuyoshi, put it up again in 1854 using traditional carpentry, so the tower standing today is a late Edo building rather than a modern replica; it is one of only twelve original keeps left in Japan. It sits at the centre of a linked cluster of smaller towers and covered corridors, a plan it shares with Himeji and Wakayama. The Meiji government demolished most of the outer works, the army held the second and third baileys until 1945, eleven listed buildings burned in the Matsuyama air raid that year, and the Tsutsui Gate was lost to arson in 1949. Even so the keep, six turrets and a long sequence of original gates and walls survive, twenty one structures are Important Cultural Properties, and the site has been a National Historic Site since 1952.","images":[{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c9/Matsuyama_castle%28Iyo%296.JPG/960px-Matsuyama_castle%28Iyo%296.JPG","caption":"The three storey keep of Matsuyama Castle in Ehime Prefecture, an 1854 rebuilding in traditional materials rather than a modern replica, rising with its linked corner turrets above the honmaru stone wall, Wikimedia Commons."}],"sources":[{"label":"Wikipedia: Matsuyama Castle (Iyo)","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Matsuyama_Castle_(Iyo)"}]},
  {"name":"Hirosaki Castle","modern":"Hirosaki, Aomori Prefecture","type":"hirayamajiro","clan":"Tsugaru","lat":40.6069444,"lon":140.4644444,"note":"Hirosaki was the seat of the Tsugaru, a family that broke away from its Nambu overlords in the 1580s. Ōura Tamenobu was rewarded by Toyotomi Hideyoshi for his part in the Odawara campaign of 1590 and took the Tsugaru surname at that time, then backed Tokugawa Ieyasu at Sekigahara and was confirmed as lord of Hirosaki Domain at 47,000 koku. He began the castle in 1603 but died in Kyoto the following year; his son Nobuhira resumed the work in 1609, stripping the older Horikoshi and Ōura castles of gates and timber to speed it along, and finished in 1611. It is a hirayamajiro on a low rise, laid out in six concentric baileys behind wide moats, roughly 600 metres east to west and 950 metres north to south. The original five-storey keep stood only sixteen years: lightning struck it in 1627 and it burned. The domain was too poor, and too wary of arousing shogunal suspicion, to replace it for nearly two centuries. The present three-storey keep, 14.4 metres tall, was raised in 1810 by the ninth daimyo Tsugaru Yasuchika on a corner turret base at the southeast rather than on the original southwest foundation. Hirosaki was never attacked. The Tsugaru surrendered it intact in 1871 and gave the grounds over as a public park in 1895, which is why so much survives: the 1810 keep, three Edo period corner turrets, five gates and a guardhouse, all National Important Cultural Properties standing on a National Historic Site. It is the northernmost of Japan's twelve surviving original keeps and the only one in Tōhoku. In 2015 the whole keep was jacked up and rolled some 70 metres inland so that the bulging stone wall beneath it could be dismantled and rebuilt, and the return move to its old base is under way in 2026.","images":[{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f3/Hirosaki-castle_Aomori_with_Sakura_blossoms.jpg/960px-Hirosaki-castle_Aomori_with_Sakura_blossoms.jpg","caption":"The original three-storey keep of Hirosaki Castle in Aomori Prefecture, built in 1810 and photographed among the park's cherry blossom in May 2007; Wikimedia Commons."}],"sources":[{"label":"Wikipedia: Hirosaki Castle","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hirosaki_Castle"}]},
  {"name":"Nagoya Castle (Owari)","modern":"Naka Ward, Nagoya, Aichi Prefecture","type":"hirajiro","clan":"Tokugawa (Owari branch); earlier Imagawa, Oda","lat":35.185556,"lon":136.898611,"note":"A small fort stood here from the 1520s, held for the Imagawa until Oda Nobuhide seized it in 1532; some accounts make it Nobunaga's birthplace. It had been derelict for a generation when Tokugawa Ieyasu ordered a great new castle on the spot in 1609 and made other lords pay for it. Twenty daimyo, many of them former enemies such as Kato Kiyomasa and Fukushima Masanori, were each assigned stretches of wall and gate under the compulsory service called tenka bushin, work that drained their treasuries while binding them publicly to the new regime. The stone bases were done in 1610, the great keep in 1612, the Honmaru palace in 1615. Ieyasu then closed down nearby Kiyosu and moved the entire town, its garrison, temples, merchants and even its buildings, to the new site in the wholesale relocation remembered as the Kiyosu-goshi; Kiyosu's keep was re-erected here as the northwest turret. The castle became the seat of Ieyasu's ninth son Yoshinao and of the Owari Tokugawa, senior of the three cadet houses from which a shogun could be drawn, and the town below grew into a major stop between the Tokaido and the Nakasendo. Its keep carried a celebrated pair of gilded shachihoko, dolphin-shaped roof ornaments so valuable that the domain melted and recast them three times to raise cash.\n\nThe Owari house submitted to the imperial side in 1868 without fighting, and the castle escaped Meiji demolition partly after foreign protests, including one from the German minister. Nagoya city took it over in 1930 and 24 buildings were declared national treasures, but an American incendiary raid on 14 May 1945 burned the great and small keeps, the Honmaru palace and the golden dolphins. Three corner turrets, several gates, the moats and the vast stone walls are original. The keep that dominates the site today is a 1959 concrete replica, shut to visitors since 2018 over earthquake safety, and the city intends to rebuild it in timber. The Honmaru palace was recreated in wood and fully opened in 2018, its Kano school wall paintings copied from surviving originals.","images":[{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/56/Nagoya_Castle_7.jpg/960px-Nagoya_Castle_7.jpg","caption":"The great keep and small keep of Nagoya Castle in Aichi Prefecture, a 1959 concrete reconstruction of the towers burned in the 1945 air raid; Wikimedia Commons."}],"sources":[{"label":"Wikipedia: Nagoya Castle","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Nagoya_Castle"}]},
  {"name":"Kanazawa Castle","modern":"Kanazawa, Ishikawa Prefecture","type":"hirayamajiro","clan":"Maeda (earlier Ikkō-ikki, then Sakuma)","lat":36.56431667,"lon":136.65922778,"note":"The site was first fortified not by a warlord but by a church. From the late fifteenth century the Ikkō-ikki, the militant congregations of the Jōdo Shinshū sect who followed the preaching of Rennyo, pushed aside the Togashi governors of Kaga and ran the province themselves. Their headquarters was the Kanazawa Gobō, a fortified temple complex on the tip of the Kodatsuno ridge between the Sai and Asano rivers. Oda Nobunaga's armies broke that arrangement, and in 1580 his general Sakuma Morimasa seized the temple site, was granted the province and began laying out the moats and baileys. Morimasa was defeated in 1583 and Maeda Toshiie took his place. Toshiie ordered a complete rebuilding in 1592 with the help of a Christian daimyō known for castle design, generally identified as Takayama Ukon, who was then living under Maeda protection. Kanazawa became the seat of the Kaga domain, assessed at over a million koku and by far the largest holding under the Tokugawa, and fourteen generations of Maeda ruled from it until 1871. Much of the castle's later history is a history of fire. The keep burned in 1602 and was never rebuilt; further fires in 1631 and 1759 swept away most of what stood, so the buildings on the site belong to repeated Edo period reconstruction rather than to the Sengoku castle. The whitish roofs are a Kanazawa signature: the tiles are lead, chosen because they do not burn, though local legend adds that they could be melted into bullets under siege. Original fabric survives in the Ishikawa Gate of 1788, the long Sanjikken Nagaya storehouse and the Tsurumaru Storehouse, all Important Cultural Properties. The army took the grounds in 1871 for the headquarters of its Ninth Division, Kanazawa University occupied part of them from 1949 to 1989, and since then the prefecture has run a patient programme of timber reconstruction using traditional joinery: the Hishi Yagura turret, Gojikken Nagaya and Hashizume-mon turret in 2001, the Kahoku Gate in 2010, the Hashizume Gate in 2015 and the Nezumita Gate in 2020. The site is now Kanazawa Castle Park, across the road from Kenrokuen garden.","images":[{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/88/Kanazawa-M-5937.jpg/960px-Kanazawa-M-5937.jpg","caption":"The Gojikken Nagaya storehouse and flanking turrets at Kanazawa Castle in Ishikawa Prefecture, a 2001 reconstruction in traditional timber joinery rather than surviving Edo period fabric, Wikimedia Commons."}],"sources":[{"label":"Wikipedia: Kanazawa Castle","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kanazawa_Castle"}]},
  {"name":"Sendai Castle (Aoba Castle)","modern":"Sendai, Miyagi Prefecture","type":"hirayamajiro","clan":"Date","lat":38.252478,"lon":140.856156,"note":"Date Masamune moved his seat here after Sekigahara, laying out the ground on Mount Aoba in the winter of 1600 with Tokugawa Ieyasu's permission and renaming the place Sendai. The hill had carried earlier forts, including the Kokubun clan's Chiyo Castle, from which the name Sendai is said to derive. Masamune chose it for its defences and for its position where the coast road and the mountain road met; the inner bailey sits about 115 metres up, closed off by cliffs to the south and east, by the Hirose river to the north, and by stone revetments as much as 15 metres high. He had a keep platform built but never raised a keep on it, relying instead on four three storey turrets and a lavish Momoyama style residence. That restraint is usually read as prudence toward a Tokugawa regime that watched the Date closely, and it makes Sendai a telling counterpoint to the tower building of the previous generation; the Spanish envoy Sebastian Vizcaino still judged it one of the strongest places in Japan. Masamune's son Tadamune completed the works and in 1639 moved the lord's residence down to a new second bailey at the foot of the hill, from which the domain was actually governed for the rest of the Edo period. The castle was never attacked. Even in the Boshin War, when Date Yoshikuni headed the northern alliance against the new imperial government, Sendai surrendered without a fight. The army took the site in 1871, fire destroyed the second bailey buildings in 1882, and the great gate with its flanking turret, National Treasures since 1931, burned in the American air raid of 10 July 1945. What survives is earthwork and stone: ramparts, moats and the tall inner bailey walls, repaired again after the 2011 earthquake. The turret beside the gate is a 1967 reconstruction, and the only original castle gate now stands elsewhere in the city, reused as the entrance to the Miyagi governor's residence. The ruins were designated a National Historic Site in 2003.","images":[{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/ef/Waki-yagura_of_Sendai_Castle_20220910b.jpg/960px-Waki-yagura_of_Sendai_Castle_20220910b.jpg","caption":"The turret flanking the great gate of Sendai Castle in Sendai, Miyagi Prefecture, a 1967 reconstruction of the National Treasure structure burned in the 1945 air raid, since the castle never had a keep; Wikimedia Commons."}],"sources":[{"label":"Wikipedia: Aoba Castle","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Aoba_Castle"}]},
  {"name":"Hagi Castle","modern":"Hagi, Yamaguchi Prefecture","type":"hirayamajiro","clan":"Mori (Choshu Domain)","lat":34.421419,"lon":131.381389,"note":"Hagi Castle was the consolation prize of a defeat. Mōri Terumoto backed the losing side at Sekigahara in 1600, and although his army never actually fought, Tokugawa Ieyasu stripped the Mōri of two thirds of their lands, including their home province of Aki and their new castle at Hiroshima, leaving them only Nagato and Suō. The Mōri asked to build a new seat at Yamaguchi or Hōfu, both on the Sanyōdō highway running from Kyoto towards Kyushu, but the shogunate refused and sent them instead to Hagi, at that time a fishing village on the Sea of Japan coast, hemmed in by hills and river marsh and far from any road that mattered. Work began in 1604 and finished in 1608. The citadel sat on Mount Shizuki, a 150 metre former island at the tip of the Abu river delta, with two walled enclosures on the sandbank below, the open sea serving as a moat on one side and a sea fed water moat on the other, and a five storey keep at the corner of the main enclosure. No army ever besieged it. Hagi matters politically rather than militarily: for more than 250 years it was the seat of Chōshū domain, whose samurai became the most dangerous enemies the shogunate had in the 1860s. In 1863, after the Shimonoseki fighting showed how exposed the coast was to foreign warships, the Mōri moved their government inland to Yamaguchi, and in 1874 the new Meiji government had the castle buildings pulled down. What survives is the ground plan in stone: the walls of the hilltop and of the main enclosure are largely intact, along with the water moat, and a gate and a corner turret have since been rebuilt. The ruins are a National Historic Site, the samurai quarter below is protected as a preserved castle town, and since 2015 the site has formed part of a UNESCO World Heritage listing.","images":[{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/b/ba/Hagi_Castle_002.jpg","caption":"The five storey keep of Hagi Castle in Hagi, Yamaguchi Prefecture, photographed before the Meiji government had it pulled down in 1874, Wikimedia Commons."}],"sources":[{"label":"Wikipedia: Hagi Castle","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hagi_Castle"}]}
];
